use web
use json
use core
use md
into settings.he
lang he

The module is namespace `settings`. It runs the settings panel, the directory list, the file sidebar, the model server panel and the Hugging Face model download of the Amni-Code page. Tags 200 to 299 are its own. It calls only `web`, `json`, `core`, `md` and the standard library, never another feature module. Text is a growable byte buffer. Html text is always escaped with `md::esc_html(out, text)` and attribute values with `md::esc(out, text)`, sizes are always written with `md::fmt_size(out, bytes)`, and no function in this module repeats what those do. Texts are joined with the standard `str`.

Listener tags, events of kind `web::EV_DOM`: `SETTINGS_BTN` 200, `OVERLAY` 201, `MODEL_CHANGE` 202, `PROVIDER_CHANGE` 203, `URL_CHANGE` 204, `MODEL_DIR_CHANGE` 205, `DIR_CHANGE` 207, `HF_KEY` 208. Fetch tags, events of kind `web::EV_FETCH`: `F_CONFIG` 210, `F_SEATS` 211, `F_MODELS` 212, `F_DIRS` 213, `F_LS` 214, `F_SAVE` 215, `F_SAVE_MODELS` 217, `F_SAVE_CHECK` 218, `F_SRV_START` 219, `F_SRV_STATUS` 220, `F_HF_SEARCH` 221, `F_HF_FILES` 222, `F_HF_DL` 223, `F_HF_PROG` 224. `S_LOG` 227 is the server log stream, whose events are `web::EV_CHUNK` and `web::EV_FETCH`. Timer tags, events of kind `web::EV_TIMER`: `T_CLOSE` 230, `T_HF_POLL` 231, `T_HF_REFRESH` 232, `T_LOG` 233.

Module state. `sb_base` is the base directory the sidebar shows, empty at first. `ls_id` is the id of the latest sidebar request, `dirs_id` the id of the latest directory list request and `dirs_base` the base it was made for, `models_id` the id of the latest models request and `models_current` the model name its reply should select, `hf_files_id` the id of the latest Hugging Face files request and `hf_repo` the repo it was made for. All ids start at -1. `seats` is a json document, `seats_loaded` says whether /api/seats has answered, `seats_arr` is the node of its "seats" array or -1, and `seat_p` is the provider whose seat status is waiting for that answer. `hf_timer` is the id of the download progress timer, -1 when there is none. `log_on` says the server log stream is wanted, `log_id` is its request id or -1, `log_timer` the id of its restart timer or -1, and `log_buf` collects its received text.

`value_of(out, id)` appends the "value" property of the element with that id to `out`, and appends nothing when the element does not exist. This block defines only `value_of`; escaping, size text and text joining are never defined in this module.

`set_value(id, text)` sets the "value" property of the element with that id to `text`.

`show(id, on)` sets the "display" style of the element with that id to "block" when `on` is true and to "none" otherwise.

`reply(out)` is used by every fetch handler. It reads the body of the fetch that the current event reports into `out` with `web::fetch_take` on `web::ev_id()`, frees that fetch with `web::fetch_free`, and returns the HTTP status from `web::ev_status()`. After a network failure the status is 0 and the body is the error message.

`reply_json(d, why)` calls `reply` with a local buffer, then parses that body with `json::parse` into the json document `d` and returns the root node. It returns -1 when the status is 0 or the body does not parse, and then `why` is cleared and receives the body for status 0 or the parse error text otherwise.

`member_text(d, n, key)` returns the text of the string member `key` of the object node `n` of the json document `d` as a view, or an empty text when the member is missing or is not a string.

`member_int(d, n, key)` returns the number member `key` of the object node `n` of `d` as a whole number, 0 when it is missing or not a number.

`member_real(d, n, key)` returns the number member `key` of the object node `n` of `d` as a real number with `json::as_f64`, 0.0 when it is missing or not a number.

`member_true(d, n, key)` returns true only when the member `key` of the object node `n` of `d` is the json value true.

`get_with(prefix, value, tag)` starts a GET fetch with tag `tag` of the url made of `prefix` followed by `value` encoded with `core::url_part(out, text)`, and returns the request id. Percent-encoding is always `core::url_part`; this module defines no encoder of its own.

`post_json(url, body, tag)` starts a POST with `web::request` to `url` with the header {"Content-Type":"application/json"}, the body `body`, tag `tag` and no timeout, and returns the request id.

`path_sep(s)` returns the byte slash when `s` contains a slash and the byte backslash otherwise.

`trim_seps(s)` returns `s` without its trailing slashes and backslashes, all of them, as a view of `s`.

`parent_of(path, sep, out)` finds the last slash or backslash in `path`. When there is none it returns false and leaves `out` alone. Otherwise it appends to `out` the part of `path` before that last separator with every slash and backslash in it replaced by the byte `sep`, or just `sep` when that part is empty, and returns true.

`join_path(out, base, sep, name)` appends `base` with one trailing slash or backslash removed when it has one, then the byte `sep`, then `name`.

`seat_render(p)` fills the element "seat-status" for provider `p` from the seats array `seats_arr` of `seats`. It looks for the first element whose "id" member text equals `p`. When there is none it sets the html to `<span class="dim">no seat info</span>`. Otherwise, with label, path, hint and get being the text members "label", "path", "hint" and "get" of that element, escaped with `md::esc_html`: when its "installed" member is true the html is `<span class="seat-ok">● ` label ` found</span> <span class="dim">` path `</span><div class="dim">` hint `</div>`, else `<span class="seat-no">○ ` label ` not installed</span><div class="dim">get it: ` get ` · ` hint `</div>`.

`seat_status(p)` takes the element "seat-status". When there is none it creates a div with id "seat-status" and class "seat-status", and when the element "cfg-provider" exists it inserts the new div into that element's parent right before that element's next sibling, which `web::js_get(provider, "nextSibling")` gives, a handle of 0 meaning at the end. When core has no provider entry for `p`, or that entry's "cli" member is not true, it sets the display of the div to "none" and stops. Otherwise it sets the display to "block" and the html to `<span class="dim">checking ` p escaped with `md::esc_html` `…</span>`. Then, when `seats_loaded` is true it calls `seat_render(p)`, else it stores `p` in `seat_p` and starts a GET of "/api/seats" with tag `F_SEATS`.

`on_seats()` handles the reply of `F_SEATS`. It parses it into `seats` with `reply_json`, sets `seats_arr` to the node of the "seats" member when that is an array and to -1 otherwise (also on any failure), sets `seats_loaded` to true, and calls `seat_render` with `seat_p`.

`apply_provider_chrome(p)` uses core's provider entry for `p`, or the one for "local" when `p` has none. The key is shown when the entry's "key" member is true and its "cli" member is not true. The url is shown when `p` is "local" or "ollama". It calls `seat_status(p)`, then with `show` it shows or hides "api-key-label" and "cfg-key" by the key rule, "base-url-label" and "cfg-url" by the url rule, and shows "model-dir-section" only when `p` is "ollama".

`save_config(tag)` posts the current settings to "/api/config" with `post_json` and the tag `tag`. It reads the values of "cfg-provider", "cfg-model", "cfg-key", "cfg-url", "cfg-dir", "cfg-mode" and "cfg-model-dir" from the page, and whether "cfg-auto" has the class "on". The model it sends is empty when the raw model starts with "Loading...", "(starting server...)", "(no models found)" or "Error loading", and otherwise the raw model. The body is one json object built with the json format functions with the members provider, model, api_key, base_url, working_dir, mode, model_dir and auto_approve (true or false), in that order.

`label_of(out, m)` appends the label that the "model_labels" member of the node `core::tables_root` of the document `core::tables` gives for the model name `m`, or `m` itself when there is no label or the label is empty.

`model_option(html, m)` appends to `html` the text `<option value="` m escaped with `md::esc` `">` the label of `m` escaped with `md::esc_html` `</option>`.

`array_has(d, arr, name)` returns true when some string element of the array node `arr` of `d` equals `name`.

`add_models(html, d, arr, skip_pinned)` appends `model_option` for every string element of the array node `arr` of `d`, in order, leaving out "adam:3b" and "adam:8b" when `skip_pinned` is true.

`load_models(current)` stores a copy of `current` in `models_current` (copy it first, it may be `models_current` itself), then starts a GET of "/api/models" with tag `F_MODELS` and keeps the request id in `models_id`.

`on_models()` handles the reply of `F_MODELS`. It takes `id` from `web::ev_id()` before calling `reply_json` into a local document, and ignores the reply when `id` differs from `models_id`. The model select is the element "cfg-model". When the reply is not json or has no "models" array, it sets the select's html to `<option>Error loading</option>` and stops. The provider `p` is the value of "cfg-provider". The list is the "models" array of the reply when it has any elements, otherwise the "models" array of core's provider entry for `p` (read with json from `core::tables`), and when there is no such array or it is empty it sets the select's html to `<option value="">` hint `</option>` and stops, the hint being "(pull a model: ollama pull llama3.2)" for "ollama", "(Adam on :7700)" for "amni" and "(type a model id)" otherwise. When `p` is "amni" the options are first "adam:3b" and "adam:8b" and then every other model of the list (`add_models` with skip), otherwise just the models of the list. `current` is `models_current`. The current model is included when it is "adam:3b" or "adam:8b" with `p` "amni", or when `array_has` finds it in the list. When `current` is not empty and is included, the select's html is the options and its "value" property is set to `current`. When `current` is not empty, not included and does not start with "(", the html is one option with value `current` escaped with `md::esc` and text `current` escaped with `md::esc_html` followed by " (custom)", followed by the other options, and the value is set to `current`. Otherwise the html is the options, the value is set to the first model (that is "adam:3b" for "amni"), and it calls `save_config` with `F_SAVE`.

`load_config()` starts a GET of "/api/config" with tag `F_CONFIG`.

`on_config()` handles the reply of `F_CONFIG`. It reads it as json with `reply_json`; when that fails it writes "loadConfig: " followed by the reason with `web::error` and stops. With `c` the root and `cwd` its "working_dir" text: it sets the text of "cwd-path" to `cwd`, or to "unknown" when `cwd` is empty. When the element "cfg-provider" exists, the provider `p` is c's "provider" text when core has a provider entry for it and "amni" otherwise; it sets that element's value to `p` and calls `apply_provider_chrome(p)`. It sets the value of "cfg-key" to c's "api_key" text, of "cfg-url" to its "base_url" text, and of "cfg-dir" to `cwd`. When c's "model_dir" text is not empty it sets the value of "cfg-model-dir" to it. It turns the class "on" of "cfg-auto" on when c's "auto_approve" is true and off otherwise, and sets the value of "cfg-mode" to c's "mode" text, or "edit" when that is empty. When c's "api_key" text is not empty it sets the text of "key-src" to "(from ENV)". Finally it calls `load_models` with c's "model" text, then `load_dirs(cwd)` and `load_sidebar(cwd)`.

`open_panel()` toggles the class "open" of the elements "settings-panel" and "settings-overlay", each on when it was off and off when it was on, then calls `load_config`.

`close_panels()` removes the class "open" from "settings-panel", "history-panel" and "settings-overlay", then calls `save_config` with `F_SAVE`.

`load_dirs(base)` stores a copy of `base` in `dirs_base` (copy first, it may be `dirs_base` itself) and starts a GET of "/api/dirs?path=" followed by the encoded base with `get_with` and tag `F_DIRS`, keeping the request id in `dirs_id`.

`on_dirs()` handles the reply of `F_DIRS`. It takes `id` from `web::ev_id()` before `reply_json` and ignores the reply when `id` differs from `dirs_id`. When the reply is not json or has no "dirs" array it changes nothing. The list is the element "dir-tree". When the array is empty it sets the list's html to `<span style="color:var(--dim)">No subdirectories</span>`. Otherwise its html is, for every string element `d` of the array, `<div class="dir-opt" style="padding:2px 6px;cursor:pointer;border-radius:4px;color:var(--text2)" data-act="settings.dir_pick" data-arg="` the full path escaped with `md::esc` `">&#128193; ` d escaped with `md::esc_html` `</div>`, where the full path is made with `join_path` from `dirs_base`, the separator `path_sep` of `dirs_base` and `d`.

`dir_pick(full)` sets the value of "cfg-dir" to `full`, then calls `load_dirs(full)` and `load_sidebar(full)`.

`dir_up()` takes the value of "cfg-dir" with `trim_seps`. When that is empty it stops. It asks `parent_of` for the parent with the separator `path_sep` of it; when there is none it stops. Otherwise it sets the value of "cfg-dir" to the parent, calls `load_dirs` and `load_sidebar` with it and then `save_config` with `F_SAVE`.

`load_sidebar(base)` stores a copy of `base` in `sb_base` (copy first, it may be `sb_base` itself). When the element "sidebar-tree" does not exist it stops. Otherwise it starts a GET of "/api/ls?path=" followed by the encoded base with `get_with` and tag `F_LS`, keeping the request id in `ls_id`.

`on_ls()` handles the reply of `F_LS`. It takes `id` from `web::ev_id()` before `reply_json` and ignores the reply when `id` differs from `ls_id`. The tree is the element "sidebar-tree". When the reply is not json or lacks a "dirs" array or a "files" array, it sets the tree's html to `<span style="color:var(--dim)">Error</span>`. Otherwise `sep` is `path_sep` of `sb_base` and `clean` is `trim_seps` of `sb_base`, and the html is `<div class="sb-path">` `sb_base` escaped with `md::esc_html` `</div>`, then when `clean` is not empty and `parent_of` gives a parent of `clean`, `<div class="sb-item sb-up" data-act="settings.sb_dir" data-arg="` the parent escaped with `md::esc` `">↑ ..</div>`, then for every string element `d` of "dirs" `<div class="sb-item sb-dir" data-act="settings.sb_dir" data-arg="` the path from `join_path` with `clean`, `sep` and `d` escaped with `md::esc` `">📁 ` d escaped with `md::esc_html` `</div>`, then for every string element `f` of "files" `<div class="sb-item sb-file" data-act="settings.sb_file" data-arg="` the path from `join_path` with `clean`, `sep` and `f` escaped with `md::esc` `">` f escaped with `md::esc_html` `</div>`.

`server_log_line(text, cls)` creates a div, adds the class `cls` to it when `cls` is not empty, sets its text to `text`, appends it to the element "server-start-log", releases its handle, and sets the "scrollTop" property of the log to its "scrollHeight" with `web::prop_num` and `web::get_num`.

`open_server_panel()` adds the class "open" to the element "server-start-overlay".

`stop_log()` sets `log_on` to false; when `log_id` is not negative it aborts that request with `web::fetch_abort` and sets `log_id` to -1; when `log_timer` is not negative it clears that timer with `web::clear_timer` and sets `log_timer` to -1.

`close_server_panel(reload)` removes the class "open" from "server-start-overlay", calls `stop_log`, and when `reload` is true calls `load_models` with the value of "cfg-model".

`log_open()` calls `stop_log`, then sets `log_on` to true, clears `log_buf` and starts the stream with `web::stream` of "/api/server-log" with method "GET", the headers {"Accept":"text/event-stream","Cache-Control":"no-cache"}, an empty body and tag `S_LOG`, keeping the request id in `log_id`.

`on_log_chunk()` handles an `S_LOG` event of kind `web::EV_CHUNK`. It takes the chunk id from `web::ev_id()` and the request id from `web::ev_status()`, reads the chunk with `web::fetch_take` and frees it with `web::fetch_free`. When `log_on` is false or the request id differs from `log_id` it stops. Otherwise it appends the chunk to `log_buf` and then, as long as `core::sse_take` gives an event with its data from `log_buf`: for the event "ready" it calls `server_log_line` with "Server is ready!" and "log-ready", sets the "animation" style of "server-start-spinner" to "none" and starts a 1500 millisecond timer with tag `T_CLOSE`; for the event "timeout" it calls `server_log_line` with "Server failed to start after 3 minutes." and "log-err" and sets the "disabled" property of "server-start-btn" to false; for an event that is empty or "message" whose data is not empty it calls `server_log_line` with the data and the class "log-err" when the data starts with "[err]", otherwise an empty class. Other events are ignored.

`on_log_end()` handles an `S_LOG` event of kind `web::EV_FETCH`. It takes the id from `web::ev_id()` and the status from `web::ev_status()` and frees that fetch. When `log_on` is false or the id differs from `log_id` it stops. Otherwise it sets `log_id` to -1, and when the status is 0 or 200 it starts a 3000 millisecond timer with tag `T_LOG` and keeps its id in `log_timer`, else it sets `log_on` to false.

`on_log_timer()` sets `log_timer` to -1 and, when `log_on` is true, clears `log_buf` and starts the same stream as `log_open` does without calling `stop_log`.

`start_server()` sets the "disabled" property of "server-start-btn" to true, sets the html of "server-start-log" to empty, calls `server_log_line` with "Requesting server start..." and an empty class, and posts "{}" to "/api/server-start" with `post_json` and tag `F_SRV_START`.

`on_server_start()` handles the reply of `F_SRV_START`. It reads it with `reply_json` into a local document. When that fails it calls `server_log_line` with "Request failed: " followed by the reason and "log-err", sets the "disabled" property of "server-start-btn" to false and stops. The status is the "status" text of the root. For "already_running" it calls `server_log_line` with "Server is already running." and "log-ready", starts a 1200 millisecond timer with tag `T_CLOSE` and stops. For "already_starting" it calls `server_log_line` with "Server is already starting — watching logs..." and an empty class. For "error" it calls `server_log_line` with "Error: " followed by the "message" text and "log-err", sets the "disabled" property of "server-start-btn" to false and stops. For "starting" it calls `server_log_line` with "Launched: " followed by the "dir" text and "log-ready". Then, unless it stopped, it calls `log_open`.

`check_and_start()` starts a GET of "/api/server-status" with tag `F_SRV_STATUS`.

`on_server_status()` handles the reply of `F_SRV_STATUS`. When `reply_json` fails it calls `load_models` with an empty text and stops. When the root's "applicable" member is not true or its "running" member is true, it calls `load_models` with an empty text and stops. Otherwise it calls `open_server_panel`; when the "reachable" member is true and the "matching" member exists and is the json value false it calls `server_log_line` with "Selected model differs from the running Amni-AI instance. Restarting with the saved selection..." and an empty class; then it calls `start_server`.

`hf_search()` takes the value of "hf-search-input" with spaces trimmed off both ends and stops when it is empty. Otherwise it sets the html of "hf-results" to `<span style="color:var(--dim)">Searching...</span>`, the html of "hf-files" to empty, and starts a GET of "/api/hf/search?q=" followed by the encoded text with `get_with` and tag `F_HF_SEARCH`.

`on_hf_search()` handles the reply of `F_HF_SEARCH`. The results element is "hf-results". When `reply_json` fails it sets its html to `<span style="color:var(--error)">Search failed</span>` and stops. When the root is not an array or has no elements it sets the html to `<span style="color:var(--dim)">No GGUF models found</span>`. Otherwise for every element `m` it appends `<div class="hf-repo" style="padding:6px 8px;cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:4px;transition:border-color .2s" data-act="settings.hf_files" data-arg="` the "id" text of `m` escaped with `md::esc` `"><div style="font-weight:600;color:var(--text)">` the id escaped with `md::esc_html` `</div><div style="color:var(--dim);font-size:11px">&#11015; ` dl ` downloads &middot; &hearts; ` likes `</div></div>`, where `dl` is the "downloads" member as a whole number: when it is at least 1000, it divided by 1000 and rounded to the nearest whole number with halves rounding up, followed by "k", otherwise the number itself; and `likes` is the "likes" member as a whole number. It sets the html of "hf-results" to that.

`hf_show_files(repo)` sets the html of "hf-files" to `<span style="color:var(--dim)">Loading files...</span>`, stores a copy of `repo` in `hf_repo` (copy first), and starts a GET of "/api/hf/files?repo=" followed by the encoded repo with `get_with` and tag `F_HF_FILES`, keeping the request id in `hf_files_id`.

`on_hf_files()` handles the reply of `F_HF_FILES`. It takes `id` from `web::ev_id()` before `reply_json` and ignores the reply when `id` differs from `hf_files_id`. The element is "hf-files". When `reply_json` fails it sets its html to `<span style="color:var(--error)">Failed to load files</span>` and stops. When the root is not an array or has no elements it sets the html to `<span style="color:var(--dim)">No GGUF files in this repo</span>`. Otherwise the html is `<div style="color:var(--accent);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:6px;font-size:11px">GGUF FILES &mdash; ` `hf_repo` escaped with `md::esc_html` `</div>` followed, for every element `f`, by `<div style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;padding:4px 8px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;margin-bottom:3px"><div style="flex:1;min-width:0"><div style="white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis" title="` the "name" text of `f` escaped with `md::esc` `">` the name escaped with `md::esc_html` `</div><div style="color:var(--dim);font-size:10px">` the "size" member of `f` taken as a real number with `member_real` and formatted with `md::fmt_size(out, bytes)`, whose bytes argument is a real `</div></div><button data-act="settings.hf_download" data-repo="` `hf_repo` escaped with `md::esc` `" data-arg="` the name escaped with `md::esc` `" style="background:var(--accent);border:none;color:#fff;padding:4px 10px;border-radius:6px;cursor:pointer;font-family:var(--font);font-size:11px;flex-shrink:0;margin-left:8px">Download</button></div>`.

`hf_clear_timer()` clears the timer `hf_timer` with `web::clear_timer` when it is not negative and sets it to -1.

`hf_download(repo, file)` sets the "display" style of "hf-progress" to "block", the text of "hf-dl-name" to `file`, the text of "hf-dl-pct" to "0%", the "width" style of "hf-dl-bar" to "0%" and the text of "hf-dl-status" to "Starting download...". Then it posts to "/api/hf/download" with `post_json`, tag `F_HF_DL` and a json object body with the members repo and file, in that order, built with the json format functions.

`on_hf_dl()` handles the reply of `F_HF_DL`. The status line is the element "hf-dl-status". When `reply_json` fails it sets the status line text to "Failed to start download" and stops. The status is the "status" text of the root. For "exists" it sets the status line text to "Already downloaded: " followed by the "path" text, the "width" style of "hf-dl-bar" to "100%" and the text of "hf-dl-pct" to "100%" and stops. For "busy" it sets the status line text to the "message" text and stops. Otherwise it sets the status line text to "Downloading to " followed by the "path" text, calls `hf_clear_timer`, and starts a 1000 millisecond timer with tag `T_HF_POLL`, keeping its id in `hf_timer`.

`on_hf_poll()` handles the timer `T_HF_POLL`. It starts the next 1000 millisecond timer with the same tag, keeping its id in `hf_timer`, then starts a GET of "/api/hf/progress" with tag `F_HF_PROG`.

`on_hf_prog()` handles the reply of `F_HF_PROG`. When `reply_json` fails it does nothing. With `downloaded` and `total` the root's "downloaded" and "total" members read with `member_real`: when `total` is more than 0, `pct` is `downloaded` divided by `total`, times 100, rounded to the nearest whole number with halves rounding up; it sets the "width" style of "hf-dl-bar" to `pct` followed by "%", the text of "hf-dl-pct" to the same, and the text of "hf-dl-status" to `downloaded` formatted with `md::fmt_size(out, bytes)` (its bytes argument is a real, so pass `downloaded` as it is), then " / ", then `total` formatted the same way. When the root's "done" member is true: it calls `hf_clear_timer`; when its "error" text is not empty it sets the text of "hf-dl-status" to "Error: " followed by that text and its "color" style to "var(--error)"; otherwise it sets the "width" style of "hf-dl-bar" to "100%", the text of "hf-dl-pct" to "100%", the text of "hf-dl-status" to "Download complete! Refreshing models...", its "color" style to "var(--accent)", and starts a 1000 millisecond timer with tag `T_HF_REFRESH`.

`add_hover_style()` creates a style element whose text is `.dir-opt:hover{background:var(--surface2)!important;color:var(--accent)!important}.hf-repo:hover{border-color:var(--accent)!important}` and appends it to the document's "head" element, found with `web::query` on `web::DOCUMENT`.

`init()` calls `add_hover_style`. It listens with `web::listen` and no flags for "click" on the element "settings-btn" with tag `SETTINGS_BTN` and on "settings-overlay" with tag `OVERLAY`, for "change" on "cfg-model" with `MODEL_CHANGE`, "cfg-provider" with `PROVIDER_CHANGE`, "cfg-url" with `URL_CHANGE`, "cfg-model-dir" with `MODEL_DIR_CHANGE` and "cfg-dir" with `DIR_CHANGE`, and for "keydown" on "hf-search-input" with `HF_KEY`. Then it calls `load_config`.

`act(verb, arg)` is called by the root for every queued action named "settings." followed by the verb. By verb: "open" calls `open_panel`; "load" calls `load_config`; "save" calls `save_config` with `F_SAVE`; "auto_toggle" turns the class "on" of "cfg-auto" off when it has it and on otherwise; "server_close" calls `close_server_panel` with false; "server_start" calls `start_server`; "server_check" calls `check_and_start`; "dir_up" calls `dir_up`; "hf_search" calls `hf_search`; "sidebar_refresh" calls `load_sidebar` with `sb_base` when `sb_base` is not empty; "dir_pick" calls `dir_pick` with `arg`; "sb_dir" calls `load_sidebar` with `arg`; "sb_file" calls `core::post` with "editor.open" and `arg`; "hf_files" calls `hf_show_files` with `arg`; "hf_download" calls `hf_download` with the "data-repo" attribute of the element `core::act_el` (read with `web::get_attr`, empty when absent) and `arg`. Any other verb is ignored.

`event(tag, kind)` is called by the root for every tag from 200 to 299 and returns nothing. For `SETTINGS_BTN` it calls `open_panel`. For `OVERLAY` it calls `close_panels`. For `MODEL_CHANGE` it calls `save_config` with `F_SAVE_CHECK` when the value of "cfg-provider" is "amni" and with `F_SAVE` otherwise. For `PROVIDER_CHANGE` it reads the value `p` of "cfg-provider", takes the provider entry of `p` or of "amni" when `p` has none, sets the value of "cfg-url" to that entry's "url" text when that is not empty, calls `apply_provider_chrome(p)`, sets the html of "cfg-model" to `<option>Loading...</option>` and calls `save_config` with `F_SAVE_MODELS`. For `URL_CHANGE` and `MODEL_DIR_CHANGE` it calls `save_config` with `F_SAVE_MODELS`. For `DIR_CHANGE` it calls `save_config` with `F_SAVE`, then `load_dirs` and `load_sidebar` with the value of "cfg-dir". For `HF_KEY` it reads the event key with `web::ev_key` and, when it is "Enter", calls `web::ev_prevent` and `hf_search`. For the fetch tags, only when `kind` is `web::EV_FETCH`: `F_CONFIG` calls `on_config`, `F_SEATS` `on_seats`, `F_MODELS` `on_models`, `F_DIRS` `on_dirs`, `F_LS` `on_ls`, `F_SAVE` only frees the reply with `reply`, `F_SAVE_MODELS` frees the reply with `reply` and calls `load_models` with an empty text, `F_SAVE_CHECK` frees the reply with `reply` and calls `check_and_start`, `F_SRV_START` calls `on_server_start`, `F_SRV_STATUS` `on_server_status`, `F_HF_SEARCH` `on_hf_search`, `F_HF_FILES` `on_hf_files`, `F_HF_DL` `on_hf_dl`, `F_HF_PROG` `on_hf_prog`. For `S_LOG`, `on_log_chunk` when `kind` is `web::EV_CHUNK` and `on_log_end` when it is `web::EV_FETCH`. For the timers, when `kind` is `web::EV_TIMER`: `T_CLOSE` calls `close_server_panel` with true, `T_HF_POLL` calls `on_hf_poll`, `T_HF_REFRESH` calls `load_models` with an empty text, `T_LOG` calls `on_log_timer`.
